We have taken the velocity values obtained between 50 and 150 revolutions to compare with the experiments. Figure 6 shows the locations of the points P1 and P2 where the velocities have been computed. Figure 6 shows the comparison between the velocities obtained by the computations and those obtained from the experiments at point PI. The magnitude of the velocities obtained from the experiments and computations are comparable but there are differences in the behavior of these velocities. There is some similarity in the velocity profiles from the experiments and the computations between 2 and 3 seconds. The two peaks between 2 and 2.5 sec in Figure 6(a) correspond to the two peaks in the same time period in Figure 6(b). In the same way, the velocities between 2.5 and 3 seconds look quite similar. However it has to be noted that in both the cases the velocity is quite unsteady. The average value of velocity over 100 revolutions in the experiments is 6.029 f t / s e c while that in the computations is 5.72 f tlsec. Figure 7 shows the velocity comparisons for a point near the ground. The magnitude of the velocities is considerably higher in this case. The averige over 100 revolutions in the experiments is 14.6 f t / s e c while the corresponding value in the computations is 13.h5 f t/sec. Even here the velocities are very unsteady but the corresponding peak values of velocities between 2 and 3 seconds seem to match pretty well. Hence, the computations are able to predict the velocities quantitatively to a reasonable accuracy.
VI. Fuselage Side Force Measurements
The next step in the experimental plan was to measure the fuselage forces. This was necessary to verify if the unsteadiness in the flow structure affects the forces on the fuselage. This experiment would also provide information about the quasi-steady forces on the fuselage.
A. Experimental Setup
The fuselage was mounted through a hole in the ground plane, and was set up to yaw about an axis that was perpendicular to the ground. A drawing of the experimental setup is shown in Fig.8. The fuselage is a fiberglass cylinder 34" long, with a hemispherical nose with a radius of 5.5".Positive side force is in the +y direction as shown in Figures 1 and 8. Positive yaw of the fuselage is clockwise when viewed from above as can be seen from Figure 8.
